hooks.doc:
A tool is about to transform a block. Cancel or replace the final state;
`None` suppresses the transformation. The block's own `tool_modified_state` part answers
first, and a block whose part cancelled the action never reaches this hook.

A player pressed a mod container's button (`clickMenuButton`), on
the server. The block entity is out of the world during the call;
mods may change it and the clicking player's carried stack.

traits.doc:
A block entity Part's typed state: one value per block entity of the Part's type,
owned by the game, not by the Part or a mod's statics.

Implement it beside [`BlockEntityPart`] and attach the Part with
[`Registrar::block_entity_part_with_state`](crate::Registrar). `BlockEntityPart`
itself stays object-safe: this trait is only for the typed calls below.

```rust,ignore
struct Press;
impl BlockEntityPartState for Press {
    type Data = u32;
    const DATA: &'static AttachmentType<u32> = &PROGRESS;
}
// In a hook with `host: &mut dyn ServerHost` and the block entity's `entity`:
let done = Press::edit_state(host, entity, |_, progress| {
    *progress += 1;
    *progress >= 200
})?;
```

Reads take `&dyn Host` and return a snapshot. Edits take `&mut dyn ServerHost`, run in
their own nested transaction and store the new value only when the closure returns.
Every call first checks that `entity` is still the block entity it named, in the
level this host is in: a replaced, removed or unloaded one refuses with
[`PartStateError::Stale`]. A block entity out of the world for its own tick still
answers, through [`BlockEntitiesHost::block_entity_identity`].

Server-side only for now: `DATA` must not sync, and a client's copy holds nothing
of it, so a client reads the default.

A data load's read-only view. The owning loader supplies its origin and
metadata; reading already-loaded data never creates this context.

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{DataLoadContext, Host};
fn game(_: &dyn Host) {}
fn load(context: &dyn DataLoadContext) { game(context); }
```

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{prelude::EntitiesHost, DataLoadContext};
fn load(context: &dyn DataLoadContext) { let _ = EntitiesHost::entity(context, 1); }
```

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{DataLoadContext, HostTransaction};
fn load(context: &mut dyn DataLoadContext) {
    let _: Result<(), ()> = context.transaction(|_, _| Ok(()));
}
```

A data map a mod registers and reads, typed (NeoForge's `DataMapType<R, T>`): its id, the
registry its entries are in (through [`DataMap::Entry`]), and how a value decodes.

Builtin maps are already registered. They return canonical codec NBT compounds,
including Float distances (with infinity preserved) and Int frequencies. This store
is server-only, shared by native and wasm hosts, not network-synced to clients.
Mod-defined maps retain the JSON-to-NBT mapping below.

Register a mod-defined map in `data_map_types` with [`DataMap::ty`], then read an entry's value with its
key's `data` ([`ItemKey::data`], [`EntityTypeKey::data`]...), or by name with
[`data_map_value`] and [`data_map_entries`] for a registry without a typed key. A value arrives as the NBT form of the JSON a file
held: a whole number is an `Int`, or a `Long` past `i32`, a fraction a `Double`, a boolean a
`Byte`, an object a `Compound` and an array a `List`. Integers outside signed
64-bit range and `null` at any nesting depth return [`DataMapError::Undecodable`]
rather than changing the value. Fractional/exponent JSON tokens use the parser's
`f64` value; empty and heterogeneous arrays retain their elements.

```
use mod_api::{DataMap, ItemKey, Tag};
/// `projecte:emc`: an item's EMC value.
struct Emc;
impl DataMap for Emc {
    type Entry = ItemKey;
    type Value = i64;
    fn id(&self) -> &str {
        "projecte:emc"
    }
    fn decode(&self, value: &Tag) -> Option<i64> {
        match value {
            Tag::Int(value) => Some(i64::from(*value)),
            Tag::Long(value) => Some(*value),
            _ => None,
        }
    }
}
assert_eq!(Emc.ty().registry, "minecraft:item");
```

An entity Part's typed state: one value per entity of the Part's type, owned by the
game, not by the Part or a mod's statics.

Implement it beside [`EntityPart`] and attach the Part with
[`Registrar::entity_part_with_state`](crate::Registrar). Only mob types (vanilla and
mod) and mod general entity types keep it; a player, any other vanilla family and an
unknown type are refused when the game loads.

```rust,ignore
struct Robot;
impl EntityPartState for Robot {
    type Data = u32;
    const DATA: &'static AttachmentType<u32> = &CHARGE;
}
// In a hook with `host: &mut dyn ServerHost` and the robot's `entity`:
let full = Robot::edit_state(host, entity, |_, charge| {
    *charge += 1;
    *charge >= 100
})?;
```

Reads take `&dyn Host` and return a snapshot; edits take `&mut dyn ServerHost`, run in
their own nested transaction and store the new value only when the closure returns, as
[`BlockEntityPartState`]'s do. Every call first checks that `entity` is of the open
world and still names a resident entity, in the level this host is in:
- a removed, unloaded or moved entity refuses with [`PartStateError::Stale`];
- an entity in another level refuses with [`PartStateError::OtherLevel`];
- an entity out of its level's lists for its own tick refuses with
  [`PartStateError::HolderBusy`].

An entity that changes level without a player riding it is rebuilt under a new id: the
old handle is `Stale` and the state arrives with the entity under its new id, so a mod
that needs continuity follows the entity's UUID. A vehicle a player rides keeps its id,
and its handle still resolves in the new level. Inside an action that is rolled back
after the entity changed level, the state stays with the entity under its new id, and
the old handle stays `Stale`. Travel fires no join or leave hook for it.

Server-side only for now: `DATA` must not sync or copy on death, and a client reads
the default.

Transactions are available only on the logical server and preserve the receiver type.

```
use mod_api::{BlockEntityError, BlockEntityRef, HostTransaction, ServerHost};
fn write(host: &mut dyn ServerHost, block: BlockEntityRef) {
    let _: Result<(), BlockEntityError> = host.transaction(|host, _tx| {
        block.set_changed(host)?;
        Ok(())
    });
}
```
```compile_fail
use mod_api::{BlockEntityError, BlockEntityRef, Host, HostTransaction};
fn write(host: &mut dyn Host, block: BlockEntityRef) {
    let _: Result<(), BlockEntityError> = host.transaction(|host, _tx| {
        block.set_changed(host)?;
        Ok(())
    });
}
```
```compile_fail
use mod_api::{ClientHost, HostTransaction};
fn client(host: &mut dyn ClientHost) {
    let _: Result<(), ()> = host.transaction(|_, _| Ok(()));
}
```

Read-only level services, like vanilla's `LevelReader`.

A level host cannot be used as a game host:
```compile_fail
use mod_api::{Host, LevelHost};
fn game(_: &dyn Host) {}
fn level(host: &dyn LevelHost) { game(host); }
```
```compile_fail
use mod_api::{ClientHost, LevelHost, ServerHost};
fn server(_: &mut dyn ServerHost) {}
fn client(_: &dyn ClientHost) {}
fn level(host: &mut dyn LevelHost) { server(host); client(host); }
```
A client-side level cannot write:
```compile_fail
use mod_api::LevelSide;
fn write(side: LevelSide<'_>) {
    if let LevelSide::Client(level) = side { level.set_block_property([0, 0, 0], "a", "b", 0); }
}
```

The view of a hook fired inside a menu, recipe or player-list loop, where no
game is at hand: the side, the registries, the settings and the mod list.
The side that owns the loop builds it once per resource generation; a hook
never builds one, and its answers never change while it is held.

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{Host, LoopContext};
fn game(_: &dyn Host) {}
fn each(context: &dyn LoopContext) { game(context); }
```

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{HostTransaction, LoopContext};
fn each(context: &mut dyn LoopContext) {
    let _: Result<(), ()> = context.transaction(|_, _| Ok(()));
}
```

The lines a data component adds to its stack's tooltip. Kept by the game from registration on;
asked only for a stack that carries the component and whose `tooltip_display` shows it, and
only while its mod is enabled. Multiple registrations for the same component all run.

The view of a hook fired while a feature generates, where no game is at
hand: on a worldgen worker thread, or on the server thread when a sapling
grows. It carries the server's registries, its settings and the mod list;
the server builds it once per resource generation, a hook never builds one,
and its answers never change while it is held. There are no writes, no
level or entity handles and no transaction: what the generating region
holds is the hook's own arguments.

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{Host, WorldgenHost};
fn game(_: &dyn Host) {}
fn generate(host: &dyn WorldgenHost) { game(host); }
```

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{HostTransaction, WorldgenHost};
fn generate(host: &mut dyn WorldgenHost) {
    let _: Result<(), ()> = host.transaction(|_, _| Ok(()));
}
```

methods.doc:
Side: server.
The mob tick costs sampled since the last call (`mob_cost_sampling`), costliest first;
clears them. Empty while nothing was sampled and on a client.

handles.doc:
Side: server.
The UUID set by `ItemEntity.setTarget`, limiting pickup to that player;
None also means an unavailable item. This is not the thrower.

Side: client.
Show or clear the crack of `breaker` on the block at `pos` in this client's own view
(`LevelRenderer.destroyBlockProgress`): stages 0..9 show a crack, any other stage removes
it. Nothing is sent to the server or to other players, and a crack not updated for 400
ticks goes away. False for a stale or foreign level or before login.

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{LevelRef, ServerHost};
fn wrong_side(level: LevelRef, host: &mut dyn ServerHost) {
    level.set_client_break_progress(host, -1, [0, 64, 0], 3);
}
```

