types.doc:
A registration phase's view. Declarations go on the hook's event; there is
no world, handle constructor, transaction or game Host at this point.

Every registration hook (`register`, `component_types`, `blocks`, `items`,
`standalone_models` and the other `Register*` hooks) reads the same frozen view,
captured once before the first of them runs, so an answer never depends on
dispatch order or on which hook asks:

- [`mods`](Self::mods): the loaded packages.
- [`registry`](Self::registry): the built-in registries, as `registries.json` lists them.
  Entries that mods declare during registration are not in it; a mod already has its own
  declarations on its event or registrar.
- [`setting`](Self::setting): the settings on disk for the loaded packages when registration
  began. Later writes are not seen.

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{Host, RegistrationContext};
fn game(_: &dyn Host) {}
fn registration(context: &RegistrationContext) { game(context); }
```

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{prelude::EntitiesHost, RegistrationContext};
fn registration(context: &RegistrationContext) { let _ = EntitiesHost::entity(context, 1); }
```

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{HostTransaction, RegistrationContext};
fn registration(context: &mut RegistrationContext) {
    let _: Result<(), ()> = context.transaction(|_, _| Ok(()));
}
```

What ends a transaction:
[`transaction_open`](crate::prelude::ModStateServerHost::transaction_open) gives it to whoever opened
the transaction (`host.transaction`'s guard), and
[`transaction_close`](crate::prelude::ModStateServerHost::transaction_close) takes it. A
write handler lent only the [`Transaction`] cannot end the transaction around it, the game's
own (a hopper's move) or another mod's.

It carries the transaction's write token and a second token, as unguessable, that only
closing checks.

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{HostTransaction, ServerHost};
fn handler(host: &mut dyn ServerHost) {
    let _: Result<(), ()> = host.transaction(|host, tx| {
        host.transaction_close(tx, true);
        Ok(())
    });
}
```

Whether a mod claims a cancellable hook.

A mod still told once an earlier one cancelled marks its method `#[listener(receive_cancelled)]`
and reads the event's `cancelled` field, which it may set back to `false`:

```
use mod_api::{ClientHost, Flow, Hooks, RenderGuiPreEvent};
#[derive(Default)]
struct Gui;
#[mod_api::horizon_mod]
impl Hooks for Gui {
    #[listener(priority = -10, receive_cancelled)]
    fn render_gui_pre(&self, _: &dyn ClientHost, event: &mut RenderGuiPreEvent) -> Flow {
        event.cancelled = false;
        Flow::Continue
    }
}
```

A hook no mod can cancel has no cancel to receive, so the option doesn't compile there:

```compile_fail
use mod_api::{ClientHost, ClientTickPreEvent, Hooks};
#[derive(Default)]
struct Ticker;
#[mod_api::horizon_mod]
impl Hooks for Ticker {
    #[listener(receive_cancelled)]
    fn client_tick_pre(&self, _: &mut dyn ClientHost, _: &mut ClientTickPreEvent) {}
}
```
